<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Description>An implementation of the Milter protocol (wire version 2), the mail filter protocol spoken by Postfix and Sendmail. Write the filter in C#: implement the callbacks for the SMTP events you care about and the library handles the framing, the option negotiation and the protocol state machine the MTA expects.</Description>
    <PackageTags>milter postfix sendmail smtp mail-filter mta email antispam</PackageTags>
    <PackageVersionTagPrefix>v</PackageVersionTagPrefix>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn),CS9107</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Josupeit.Mail.Milter.IntegrationTests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Josupeit.Mail.Milter.UnitTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="ConfigureAwaitChecker.Analyzer" Version="5.0.0.1">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Nito.AsyncEx.Coordination" Version="[5,6)" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.Interactive.Async" Version="[6.0.1,7)" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.IO.Pipelines" Version="[8,9)" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.Linq.Async" Version="[6.0.1,7)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Known problem, deliberately left alone.

      This is a plain reference rather than an Analyzer one, because the library itself needs the marker
      attributes (MilterContextMarker, MilterCallbackMarker) that live in the generator project. The price
      is that consumers inherit all of it: Josupeit.Mail.Milter.Generator.dll lands in the output directory
      and Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp and .Common appear in deps.json. A runtime library has no business
      dragging the compiler along.

      The fix is to move the marker attributes into their own small assembly, or to declare them here and
      let the generator go on matching them by assembly and type name - which is what IsDescribedBy already
      does, so the generator would not have to change. It is not done because the generator may be dropped
      altogether (see CLAUDE.md); that call should come first, since dropping it removes the problem.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Josupeit.Mail.Milter.Generator\Josupeit.Mail.Milter.Generator.csproj" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Second half of the same problem. The generator is not a package, so a dependency on it would be a
    dependency on something that will never exist on nuget.org - hence PrivateAssets above. But this
    assembly's metadata still names the marker attribute types, so the assembly carrying them has to
    travel inside the package or the consumer resolves a reference to nothing.

    Both lines disappear along with the ProjectReference itself.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetsForTfmSpecificBuildOutput>$(TargetsForTfmSpecificBuildOutput);IncludeGeneratorAssemblyInPackage</TargetsForTfmSpecificBuildOutput>
    <TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>$(TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage);IncludeGeneratorAsAnalyzerInPackage</TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="IncludeGeneratorAssemblyInPackage" DependsOnTargets="ResolveReferences">
    <ItemGroup>
      <BuildOutputInPackage Include="@(ReferencePath)" Condition="'%(Filename)' == 'Josupeit.Mail.Milter.Generator'" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    The same assembly a second time, under analyzers/. The lib/ copy above only satisfies the reference
    this assembly's metadata makes to the marker attributes - it is never loaded by the compiler, so on
    its own the generator would ship with the package and never run in a consumer's build.
  -->
  <Target Name="IncludeGeneratorAsAnalyzerInPackage" DependsOnTargets="ResolveReferences">
    <ItemGroup>
      <TfmSpecificPackageFile Include="@(ReferencePath)" Condition="'%(Filename)' == 'Josupeit.Mail.Milter.Generator'">
        <PackagePath>analyzers/dotnet/cs</PackagePath>
      </TfmSpecificPackageFile>
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
